WEBVTT

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:02.159
You send the exact same prompt twice.

00:00:02.354 --> 00:00:07.491
On the second request, LMCache finds all 257 tokens.

00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:12.320
But vLLM plans to load only 256.

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:15.202
Why leave one out?

00:00:15.394 --> 00:00:18.557
Start with how a model generates its first output token.

00:00:18.749 --> 00:00:22.091
The model splits your text into tokens,

00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:25.517
then processes the input. That's prefill.

00:00:25.709 --> 00:00:31.109
At the last input position, the model produces a score for every candidate in its vocabulary.

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:33.925
Those scores are called logits.

00:00:34.131 --> 00:00:39.702
vLLM uses them to pick the first output token.

00:00:39.907 --> 00:00:42.504
The KV cache holds a different piece of the computation.

00:00:42.696 --> 00:00:46.162
Think of it as notes that each attention layer takes while reading the prompt.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:51.123
At a new position, the layer can look back at those notes.

00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:56.623
If a new request starts with the same tokens, the model can reuse the notes and skip that earlier work.

00:00:56.828 --> 00:01:00.899
The notes don't contain the logits.

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:07.096
To get those, the model can run the last prompt token again, using the earlier KV.

00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:09.583
Now let's repeat a request.

00:01:09.788 --> 00:01:14.262
The first time, LMCache saves one chunk of 256 tokens

00:01:14.262 --> 00:01:17.346
and a one-token tail.

00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:23.728
Then we clear just vLLM's local prefix cache and send the same prompt again.

00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:30.280
The local cache misses. LMCache still has all 257 tokens.

00:01:30.473 --> 00:01:36.031
The plan is to load the first 256 and run the last one in the next forward pass.

00:01:36.236 --> 00:01:37.873
Here's where the lengths get out of sync.

00:01:38.078 --> 00:01:44.741
The scheduler gives the load enough GPU blocks for one full chunk.

00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:48.938
But retrieve still looks up objects for the original 257 tokens.

00:01:49.144 --> 00:01:51.452
The full chunk has a destination.

00:01:51.657 --> 00:01:56.754
The tail has none.

00:01:56.946 --> 00:02:03.754
When the tail handler asks for a GPU block, it throws, and the whole retrieval fails.

00:02:03.946 --> 00:02:11.412
Zoom in on the boundary. A chunk holds 256 tokens. A GPU block holds 64.

00:02:11.604 --> 00:02:20.109
When the prompt is exactly 256 tokens, we reserve the last one. Loading the first 255 still takes four blocks.

00:02:20.302 --> 00:02:30.438
At 512, the load plan ends at 511. That still takes eight blocks. Neither aligned length has a tail object.

00:02:30.644 --> 00:02:37.175
At 257, the tail has just one token. That's the one reserved for forward.

00:02:37.381 --> 00:02:44.175
Add one more token, and the prompt is 258. We reserve the last one, and token 257 still needs its KV loaded.

00:02:44.381 --> 00:02:55.004
That one extra position requires a fifth block. Only a one-token tail can be stored yet have no destination in this load plan.

00:02:55.196 --> 00:02:57.728
Now turn off save_unfull_chunk.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:07.912
With the local cache still empty in our example, the 257 and 258 token prompts both hit only the first full chunk. Lookup stops at 256.

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:17.386
Both loads use four blocks. One request runs one token from there, and the other runs two.

00:03:17.578 --> 00:03:21.596
The full chunk is still reused, and retrieve has no tail to handle.

00:03:21.802 --> 00:03:29.359
An aligned prompt of 256 or 512 can still hit in full, with the last token reserved for forward.

00:03:29.552 --> 00:03:33.004
The pull request adds a check inside retrieve.

00:03:33.209 --> 00:03:37.131
If the tail object exists and every group has an empty list of destination blocks for it,

00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:40.728
skip the tail and keep the full chunks.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.728
If some groups have blocks and others don't, the existing check still raises.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.215
The token count also leaves out the skipped tail.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.320
So when you see a full hit, ask three questions.

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:55.833
How much was stored?

00:03:56.038 --> 00:03:57.702
How much are we loading?

00:03:57.907 --> 00:03:59.320
And what still runs?

00:03:59.512 --> 00:04:05.017
Here it's 257, 256, and that very last token.

00:04:05.223 --> 00:04:12.800
Once the load succeeds, the forward pass produces the logits and generation can begin.
